<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eight-legged essay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1480s–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Baguwen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The eight-legged essay was the style of essay written in the imperial examinations of the Ming and Qing dynasties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, intended to test candidates' merits for government service through Confucian thought and knowledge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Four Books and Five Classics</a>. Its eight legs were the sections of a fixed structure, from breaking open the topic to the conclusion, with rules for the number of sentences and words. The term first appeared in the early reign of the Chenghua Emperor, and the form was required in the examinations from the 1480s. Widely criticised under the Qing for its rigid, formulaic character, it was permanently abolished in 1905 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eight-legged-essay.jpg" alt="Eight-legged essay" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 三猎 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:状元卷.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
